  "notes": [
    "Every number here was re-derived from the raw traces by scripts/phase12-8-report.py, not read from the live session's transcript. A transcript cannot be re-checked and a number in it cannot be traced to the samples that produced it.",
    "Sum(hops) == t_last - t_first is arithmetic, not a check: differences of stored stamps telescope. Coverage, the two instruments, the sign gates and the count identities are what can fail.",
    "p99.9 appears only where the gap-overlap census does, and then twice: over all chains and over the chains that were running throughout."
  ],
  "status": "NOT QUOTABLE: the CPU ran at the powersave governor for this entire boot",
  "governor_during_run": "powersave",
  "why_the_numbers_are_wrong": "Every hop came back 4-6x slower than the same code under WSL2, uniformly, across hops that share no code. A uniform slowdown across unrelated paths is the clock the code ran at, not a property of the code. The harness that produced this run recorded the governor and did not gate on it; it does now.",
  "what_this_run_DID_establish": [
    "the machine holds a CPU: not one gap over 100 us on either pinned core across 20 s, 10 per second over 10 us, worst 27 us -- against ~3,000 per second and a worst of 2.57 ms under WSL2",
    "the runs repeat: the headline moved 2.0% across ten runs, against 13-18% under WSL2, and 8 of 12 hops came back poolable",
    "the census is clean: 0 of 1,200 chains descheduled, and no chain above 3x the median",
    "the clock is fine: clocksource=tsc, cross-core offset unmeasurable, bounded at 50 ns"
  ],
  "watch_on_the_next_boot": "cross-process hops came back up to 17 us NEGATIVE against a 58.6 ns clock bound. That cannot be the clock. It may be the powersave governor stretching both processes unevenly, or the exchange having been pinned to cpu0 where Linux points interrupts. Both are fixed for the next run; if it survives them it is a real finding and not an artefact."
